00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:14.080
For years now, it's been known that if you are talking to suspected terrorists, your communications are subject to surveillance.

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:18.080
Talking to suspected terrorists is basically these lawyers' job description.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:24.720
And one of these attorneys, Candace Gorman, said that her phone started acting weird when she started taking Guantanamo cases in 2006.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:29.600
When she would miss a call, two numbers would appear on her phone instead of one.

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:37.500
And I could actually call back the other call that was missed, and it would be picked up by what was called a special operator.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.920
And I brought my phone to a service.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:45.000
I'm trying to think of what the guy was called.

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:50.960
And the first thing he did when he looked at it was said, do you have any reason to believe that your phone is being tapped?

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:53.280
And so what has that done to your communications?

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:56.180
Like, what do you do differently because of that, if anything?

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:59.140
Well, I stopped taking cases back in 2007.

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:13.680
If I was being monitored, which I believed I was, and I believe I still am, I knew I couldn't give confidentiality to my clients, which is one of the main things attorneys have to promise their clients.

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:18.820
Several lawyers told me very believable stories about being monitored while talking about their cases.

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:27.940
In February, you may have read, a government official admitted that there are microphones hidden in the rooms at Guantanamo where lawyers have their supposedly confidential conversations with their clients.

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:30.460
But of course, monitoring may go way beyond that.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:37.580
David Nevin represents the man who's called the architect of the 9-11 attacks, He attacks Khalil Sheikh Mohammed, and he represents other detainees as well.

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:43.620
He says that there's no way to really know the extent that private, non-work conversations are being listened in on.

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:44.560
But still.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:49.340
If you're being listened in on, how do you talk to people?

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:51.400
How do you talk to the people who are closest to you?

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:56.900
You say that silly, loving thing that you might have said.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.680
I mean, will you just keep certain private information off just because you just feel like, I don't want them to know that.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:28.470
that's that's not for them absolutely absolutely can you think of a time when you did that an example that isn't so personal that you can't say it on the radio so I'm asking you to zero in on something that's too personal for government eavesdroppers but not so personal you don't mind saying it to to, like, two and a half million people.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:30.340
Right, exactly.

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:33.280
No, nothing like that comes to mind.

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:39.380
You know, I find myself being more reserved on the phone than I otherwise might be.

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:43.000
Ramzi Qasim has been representing Guantanamo DT&amp;E since 2005.

